Forward the skill and subagent name from PostToolUse

`tool_complete` carries only `tool_name`, so a consumer sees `Skill` and
`Agent` with no idea which skill or which subagent ran. Claude Code already
puts the names in the hook input — `tool_input.skill` for `Skill`,
`tool_input.subagent_type` for `Agent`/`Task`.

Forward those two keys by name rather than the whole `tool_input`. They
identify a local capability, not user content, so a consumer that promises
never to store prompts, responses or tool inputs can keep that promise. The
original key is preserved, so a reader can still tell a skill from a subagent.

`on-permission-request.sh` already sends a full `tool_input`, so the field is
part of the v1 payload and no protocol version bump is needed.

Applies to warpdotdev/claude-code-warp at e0e18e1.

--- a/plugins/warp/scripts/on-post-tool-use.sh
+++ b/plugins/warp/scripts/on-post-tool-use.sh
@@ -16,8 +16,19 @@
 INPUT=$(cat)

 TOOL_NAME=$(echo "$INPUT" | jq -r '.tool_name // empty' 2>/dev/null)

+# Only the name of the capability being invoked, never the rest of tool_input:
+# `Skill` names its skill, `Agent`/`Task` name their subagent type. The key is
+# kept so a reader can tell the two apart.
+TOOL_CAPABILITY=$(echo "$INPUT" | jq -c '
+    if (.tool_input.skill // "") != "" then {skill: .tool_input.skill}
+    elif (.tool_input.subagent_type // "") != "" then {subagent_type: .tool_input.subagent_type}
+    else empty end' 2>/dev/null)
+
+CAPABILITY_ARGS=()
+if [ -n "$TOOL_CAPABILITY" ]; then
+    CAPABILITY_ARGS=(--argjson tool_input "$TOOL_CAPABILITY")
+fi
+
 BODY=$(build_payload "$INPUT" "tool_complete" \
-    --arg tool_name "$TOOL_NAME")
+    --arg tool_name "$TOOL_NAME" "${CAPABILITY_ARGS[@]}")

 "$SCRIPT_DIR/warp-notify.sh" "warp://cli-agent" "$BODY"
